# Cursor Rules for wipshmup2 – Godot 4.5 Shmup

This file defines enforceable rules for Cursor agents. Detailed guidance lives in `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`.

## Scope & Precedence
- Root rules are mandatory. Use modular rules for details; do not duplicate.
- Prefer minimal, reversible edits; never crash on bad data.

## Cursor Execution Policy
- Search-first: use semantic search to understand affected systems before edits.
- Prefer parallel read-only queries; only sequence when outputs are dependent.
- Always provide brief status updates when running tools and before/after batches.
- End turns with a short high-signal summary of changes/answers when appropriate.
- Prefer absolute paths in tool calls; avoid interactive prompts (use non-interactive flags).

### Code Citation & Proposals
- When referencing existing code, use CODE REFERENCES with the exact syntax:
  - ```startLine:endLine:filepath
    // code content
    ```
- For new/proposed code, use standard markdown code blocks with a language tag (e.g., ```gdscript, ```bash).
- Never mix formats, never include line numbers inside code blocks, never indent the opening backticks.

### Safety & Ops
- Do not run long-lived commands in the foreground; background them when needed.
- Keep edits minimal and reversible; prefer guard clauses to prevent crashes.

## Core Integration Rules
- Spawns: use `EntityFactory` (pooled). Do not instantiate scenes ad-hoc.
- Events: use `EventBus`; avoid cross-calls between systems.
- State: read/write via `GameState` only; do not duplicate.
- Templates: use Enemy/Boss/Stage managers; keep params in data.

## Godot Safety Cribsheet
- Strong typing and `@export` for inspector-tunable values.
- Use `call_deferred()` for lifecycle-sensitive adds/removes.
- Guard with `is_instance_valid()`; prefer `get_node_or_null()`.
- Validate signals; prevent duplicates; disconnect on cleanup.
- Re-check validity after `await` points.

## Performance & Pooling
- Reuse pooled instances (toggle visibility vs `queue_free()` where suitable).
- Respect bullet caps and rate limits; degrade gracefully under load.
- Avoid heavy per-frame allocations; cache references.
- Avoid `get_nodes_in_group()` in hot paths; batch or cache instead.

## Pre-Edit Checklist
- Read relevant manager/system code; confirm spawn/event/state paths.
- Keep CRT/bullet readability shaders intact; tune via exported params only.
- Consider rate limits and pooling implications; add guard clauses and safe defaults.
- Ensure edits preserve event-driven decoupling; no ad-hoc cross-calls.
- Update related docs/configs if behavior changes.

## Protected Areas
- CRT and dithering shaders.
- Autoload registrations in `project.godot`.
- Core factory/manager interfaces.

## Mini Glossary
- `EntityFactory`: centralized spawning and object pooling for player/enemies/bullets.
- `EventBus`: global event hub for combat/visual/audio/game flow.
- `GameState`: lives, bombs, score, flags, chain; emits changes.
- Template Managers: `EnemyTemplateManager`, `BossTemplateManager`, `StageTemplateManager`.

